{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8c7f657fc8777936", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tLLM4OSC: Profile-Bound Natural Language Control\n2\twith Deterministic Validation for Open Sound Control\n3\tYuan-Yi Fan\n4\twww.yuanyifan.com\n5\tLos Angeles\n6\tyyf@yuanyifan.com\n7\t\n8\tarXiv:2607.26024v1 [cs.HC] 28 Jul 2026\n9\t\n10\tAbstract\n11\tOpen Sound Control (OSC) is the dominant wire protocol for real-time parametric control in professional audio, live performance, and virtual production. Large language models can emit plausible\n12\tOSC, but they hallucinate addresses, mishandle type tags, and fail under paraphrase—unacceptable\n13\tin show-critical contexts. We present LLM4OSC, a local-first architecture in which models propose structured intent JSON over a human-reviewed device profile, and deterministic code validates,\n14\tclamps, and encodes before any UDP send. We introduce a frozen evaluation harness with CI gates\n15\ton wrong-send rate: mismatches that would still pass validation and transmit. On a Max/MSP hero\n16\tprofile (12 patterns; 8 literal + 8 paraphrase + 4 refusal cases), after profile tag enrichment, symbolic\n17\tslot fill, NL refine, and a retrieval confidence gate, backends B0–B3 all pass frozen gates (100%\n18\tsemantic accuracy, 0% wrong-send). B0 (rules) remains the production default at ∼0.05 ms; LLM\n19\tbackends remain ∼3–4 s. Historical few-shot B2 accuracy of 62.5% rises to 100% on this suite\n20\tonly after symbolic post-processing—not because the 0.5B model alone becomes show-safe. We\n21\targue for propose–validate–send and wrong-send rate as first-class metrics for language-to-control\n22\tsystems.\n23\t\n24\tKeywords. Open Sound Control, tool use, structured generation, safety-critical HCI, local inference,\n25\tparametric control, creative AI.\n26\t\n27\t1\n28\t\n29\tIntroduction\n30\t\n31\tOSC [6] encodes real-time control as UDP messages: an address path (e.g. /gain), type tags (e.g.\n32\tf), and typed arguments. Max/MSP, Ableton Live, QLab, TouchDesigner, Unreal Engine, and lighting\n33\tconsoles each expose distinct address tables. Bridging operator intent to correct bytes does not scale\n34\twith show complexity.\n35\tMCP2OSC [1] shows that LLMs coupled with structured tool interfaces can drive parametric control.\n36\tProduction use additionally demands: (1) reproducibility (same command → same bytes); (2) closedworld safety (no invented addresses); (3) graceful refusal; (4) local operation; (5) paraphrase robustness.\n37\tWe treat NL→OSC as schema-constrained resolution over a versioned device profile:\n38\tdevice profile P + utterance u → intent JSON I (proposed)\n39\t→ validate → clamp → encode → OSC\n40\t\n41\tContributions. (1) Intent and device-profile JSON contracts with manual provenance; (2) Tier 3 deterministic validate/clamp/encode/send; (3) Four NL backends (B0–B3) plus NL refine and a retrieval gate\n42\ton the LLM path; (4) An evaluation harness with wrong-send rate and literal–paraphrase gap (Track C);\n43\t(5) An open MIT replication package with frozen goldens, CI, and scorecards.\n44\t\n45\t1\n46\t\n47\t\f2\n48\t\n49\tRelated Work\n50\t\n51\tOSC [6] remains the standard wire protocol for real-time music and multimedia control. MCP2OSC [1]\n52\tdemonstrates parametric control by natural language via structured tool interfaces (NeurIPS Creative AI\n53\tTrack). Parallel work on tool-using LMs [5] shows models can learn when and how to call APIs; we\n54\tinstead bind proposals to a closed device profile and refuse out-of-world calls.\n55\tWe frame warm-path pattern selection as lightweight retrieval over the profile catalog, in the spirit\n56\tof retrieval-augmented generation [3], but with token-overlap ranking rather than dense retrievers. NL\n57\trefine and the retrieval gate follow the propose–then-revise pattern of Self-Refine [4], except that feedback and overrides come from deterministic profile policy (tag scores, slot fill, refusal reasons), not from\n58\tanother LM pass. We extend the MCP2OSC line with an auditable send gate, versioned profiles, frozen\n59\twrong-send evaluation, and local inference. For optional domain adaptation we use LoRA [2] on synthetic profile-bound (NL, intent) pairs filtered through Tier 3, holding out frozen goldens. We optimize\n60\twrong-send rate: mismatches that pass validation and would transmit.\n61\t\n62\t3\n63\t\n64\tSystem\n65\t\n66\t3.1\n67\t\n68\tDevice Profile and Intent\n69\t\n70\tA profile P = (D, V, M) binds device id D, version V , and patterns M. Each pattern defines\n71\tpattern id, OSC address, type tags, slots, ranges, and retrieval metadata (tags, description).\n72\tProfiles are human-committed; runtime binds to an explicit profile version. The profile is the\n73\tclosed world: no address outside M may be sent.\n74\tAn intent is either a success (kind:intent with pattern fields and args) or a refusal (kind:refusal\n75\twith enumerated reason). Reasons include unknown pattern, ambiguous pattern, missing slot,\n76\tand out of range.\n77\t\n78\t3.2\n79\t\n80\tTier 3 Execution\n81\t\n82\tEngine E : (P, I) → bytes | reject validates device/version and pattern consistency, clamps numeric args, encodes OSC deterministically, and sends UDP. Design invariant: probabilistic components\n83\tpropose; Tier 3 decides and sends. No ML sits between intent JSON and wire bytes.\n84\t\n85\t3.3\n86\t\n87\tRetrieval, Refine, and Gate\n88\t\n89\tPatterns are ranked by token overlap: tags (weight ×3) plus description [3]. Top-k=8 patterns form\n90\tLLM context; the same ranking drives B0.\n91\tSlot fill deterministically parses percents, word numbers (half→ 0.5), note names (A440), pancenter phrases, and transport keywords from the utterance.\n92\tNL refine (B1–B3). After the LLM proposes JSON [5], retrieval may override pattern id when\n93\ta higher-scoring pattern dominates, and slot fill re-extracts args from the original NL [4]. Refine runs on\n94\tall LLM backends, not only LoRA.\n95\tRetrieval gate (B1–B3). After refine, apply retrieval gate reapplies B0 policy: top score\n96\t0 → unknown pattern; tied tops → ambiguous pattern; unfillable required slots → missing slot;\n97\tchosen pattern with score 0 → refuse. This closes OOV wrong-sends (e.g. “boost the bass band by 3db”).\n98\t\n99\t3.4\n100\t\n101\tBackends and LoRA\n102\t\n103\tTable 1 summarizes backends. LoRA training expands templates over P (∼270 train / 67 val rows),\n104\texcludes frozen goldens, requires Tier 3-pass labels, and fine-tunes with r=8, α=16, one epoch on\n105\tApple MPS. Adapter weights are gitignored; a model card records the recipe.\n106\t\n107\t2\n108\t\n109\t\fTable 1: NL backends. Refine and gate apply to B1–B3.\n110\tID\n111\t\n112\tMechanism\n113\t\n114\tRole\n115\t\n116\tB0\n117\tB1\n118\tB2\n119\tB3\n120\t\n121\tRetrieval + slot fill\n122\tQwen2-0.5B zero-shot + refine + gate\n123\tB1 + 8 few-shot + refine + gate\n124\tB1 + LoRA + refine + gate\n125\t\n126\tProduction default\n127\tPre-train floor\n128\tPrompt ceiling\n129\tOptional fine-tune\n130\t\n131\tTable 2: Track C overview (post-gate). All backends pass frozen gates. Harness: demo backend=b0,\n132\tlora recommended=false.\n133\tBackend\n134\tLit.\n135\tPara. Gap WS (para)\n136\tp50\n137\tB0\n138\tB1+refine+gate\n139\tB2+refine+gate\n140\tB3+refine+gate\n141\t\n142\t4\n143\t\n144\t100%\n145\t100%\n146\t100%\n147\t100%\n148\t\n149\t100%\n150\t100%\n151\t100%\n152\t100%\n153\t\n154\t0\n155\t0\n156\t0\n157\t0\n158\t\n159\t0%\n160\t0%\n161\t0%\n162\t0%\n163\t\n164\t0.05 ms\n165\t∼3.7 s\n166\t∼4.0 s\n167\t∼3.6 s\n168\t\n169\tEvaluation\n170\t\n171\tDevice: Max/MSP profile prof 20260610 mvp0 (12 patterns). Suites: 8 literal NL, 8 paraphrase\n172\tNL, 4 refusals (unknown, OOV EQ, missing slot, ambiguous). Default llm4osc score suite is\n173\tliteral+refusal (CI gate); paraphrase is Track C.\n174\tMetrics: semantic accuracy (match on pattern id, address, args); wrong-send rate (mismatch\n175\tand Tier 3 dry-run would succeed); refusal recall; reproducibility; latency p50/p95. Release gate:\n176\twrong-send = 0% and semantic accuracy ≥ 90%. Gap:\n177\tgapθ = (accliteral − accparaphrase ) × 100.\n178\t\n179\t5\n180\t\n181\tResults\n182\t\n183\tNumbers from frozen scorecards generated 2026-07-07 (track c.json), after tags/slots, NL refine,\n184\tand the retrieval gate. CI: 111 pytest cases pass (2 skipped).\n185\t\n186\t5.1\n187\t\n188\tWhy B2 Moved from 62.5% to 100%\n189\t\n190\tThe 2026-06-28 Track C baseline scored raw B2 (few-shot → intent → Tier 3) at 62.5% literal / 62.5%\n191\tparaphrase with non-zero wrong-send. Three symbolic changes explain the jump (Table 3):\n192\tParaphrase recovery is driven by tags/slots + refine (no refusals in that suite). Literal gates require the\n193\tretrieval gate: without it, LLM backends (including LoRA) still wrong-sent on OOV/ambiguous/missingslot goldens. On this small closed suite, post-LLM policy can make B1/B2 match B3 accuracy—a feature of the architecture, not evidence that raw 0.5B few-shot is show-safe. Latency still favors B0 by\n194\t∼105 ×.\n195\t\n196\t5.2\n197\t\n198\tHistorical Ablation and Suite Detail\n199\t\n200\tLoRA-only (no refine) reached paraphrase 62.5% with 37.5% wrong-send—the same failure class as\n201\tearly B2 until symbolic refine. Literal suite (8 NL + 4 refusal) and paraphrase suite (8 NL) both show\n202\t100% semantic accuracy and 0% wrong-send for B0–B3 after the gate; refusal recall is 100% on the\n203\tliteral suite.\n204\t\n205\t3\n206\t\n207\t\fTable 3: Causes of the B2 accuracy/gate recovery.\n208\tChange\n209\t\n210\tEffect on B2\n211\t\n212\tProfile tags + slot\n213\tparsers\n214\tNL refine on B1–B3\n215\t\n216\tUsable retrieval; args independent of LLM unit parsing\n217\t\n218\tRetrieval gate\n219\t\n220\tOverrides wrong pattern id; re-fills args → paraphrase 62.5% → 100%\n221\tAligns refusals with B0 → literal gates pass\n222\tTable 4: Historical snapshots.\n223\t\n224\t5.3\n225\t\n226\tSnapshot\n227\t\n228\tB2 L/P\n229\t\n230\tGates\n231\t\n232\tNotes\n233\t\n234\t2026-06-28\n235\t2026-06-29\n236\t2026-07-07\n237\t\n238\t62.5/62.5\n239\t—\n240\t100/100\n241\t\n242\tfail\n243\t—\n244\tpass\n245\t\n246\tRaw LLM\n247\tB0 100%/100%; B3 refusals fail\n248\t+ retrieval gate\n249\t\n250\tFindings\n251\t\n252\tF1. Wrong-send rate discriminates backends before policy: historical B2 beat B1 on accuracy but still\n253\twrong-sent. F2. Profile engineering can close paraphrase without LoRA (lora recommended=false).\n254\tF3. Symbolic refine explains the B2 jump from 62.5% to 100% on this suite. F4. The retrieval gate is\n255\trequired for refusal safety on the LLM path. F5. Equal accuracy ̸= equal production choice: prefer B0\n256\tfor live control on latency.\n257\t\n258\t6\n259\t\n260\tDiscussion\n261\t\n262\tLLM4OSC treats NL control as interface compilation over a closed API. On a tiny frozen suite, policy\n263\t(profile + refine + gate) can dominate weights (few-shot / LoRA). Put truth in the contract; measure\n264\twrong-sends; refuse when retrieval is empty.\n265\tLimitations: small benchmark (8+8+4, one device); profile-engineering confound; refine+gate can\n266\tmask weak LLM proposals; LLM latency ∼3–4 s vs < 50 ms target; no user study; synthetic LoRA data\n267\t(∼270 rows).\n268\tFuture work: expand to 100–500+ stratified cases and a second device; ablate refine vs gate vs\n269\tLoRA on larger OOV suites; wrong-send–aware training; MCP server surface; multi-arg/bundle schema\n270\tv2.\n271\t\n272\t7\n273\t\n274\tConclusion\n275\t\n276\tWe presented LLM4OSC for profile-bound NL→OSC with deterministic Tier 3 validation. Wrong-send\n277\trate exposes unsafe successes that accuracy hides; enriched profiles and slot parsers can meet paraphrase\n278\tgates without LoRA; historical few-shot B2 scores reach 100% only after NL refine; a retrieval confidence gate is required for refusal safety on the LLM path. B0 remains the live-control default. Code,\n279\tschemas, goldens, and scorecards are released under MIT.\n280\t\n281\tData and Code Availability\n282\tSource code, frozen goldens, scorecards, and evaluation documentation: https://github.com/\n283\tyyf/LLM4OSC (MIT).\n284\t\n285\t4\n286\t\n287\t\fA\n288\t\n289\tSuccess Intent Example\n290\t\n291\t{\"schema_version\":\"1.0\",\"kind\":\"intent\",\n292\t\"device_id\":\"max-msp\",\n293\t\"profile_version\":\"prof_20260610_mvp0\",\n294\t\"pattern_id\":\"gain_set\",\"address\":\"/gain\",\n295\t\"type_tags\":\"f\",\"args\":[0.5]}\n296\t\n297\tB\n298\t\n299\tReproduction\n300\t\n301\tgit clone https://github.com/yyf/LLM4OSC\n302\tcd LLM4OSC && python3 -m venv .venv\n303\tsource .venv/bin/activate\n304\tpip install -e \".[dev]\"\n305\tpytest\n306\t# 111 passed, 2 skipped\n307\tllm4osc score\n308\tllm4osc score --suite paraphrase\n309\t\n310\tFor Track C (B1–B3): pip install -e \".[dev,llm]\", then llm4osc serve and llm4osc\n311\tscore-compare --backends b0,b1,b2,b3.\n312\t\n313\tReferences\n314\t[1] Yuan-Yi Fan. MCP2OSC: Parametric control by natural language. In The Thirty-ninth Annual\n315\tConference on Neural Information Processing Systems Creative AI Track: Humanity, 2025. URL\n316\thttps://openreview.net/forum?id=XLdFf7Zarw.\n317\t[2] Edward J. Hu, Yelong Shen, Phillip Wallis, Zeyuan Allen-Zhu, Yuanzhi Li, Shean Wang, Lu Wang,\n318\tand Weizhu Chen. LoRA: Low-rank adaptation of large language models, 2021. URL https:\n319\t//arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685.\n320\t[3] Patrick Lewis, Ethan Perez, Aleksandra Piktus, Fabio Petroni, Vladimir Karpukhin, Naman Goyal,\n321\tHeinrich Küttler, Mike Lewis, Wen-tau Yih, Tim Rocktäschel, Sebastian Riedel, and Douwe Kiela.\n322\tRetrieval-augmented generation for knowledge-intensive NLP tasks. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, 2020. URL https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401.\n323\t[4] Aman Madaan, Niket Tandon, Prakhar Gupta, Skyler Hallinan, Luyu Gao, Sarah Wiegreffe, Uri\n324\tAlon, Nouha Dziri, Shrimai Prabhumoye, Yiming Yang, Shashank Gupta, Bodhisattwa Prasad Majumder, Katherine Hermann, Sean Welleck, Amir Yazdanbakhsh, and Peter Clark. Self-refine: Iterative refinement with self-feedback. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, 2023.\n325\tURL https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.17651.\n326\t[5] Timo Schick, Jane Dwivedi-Yu, Roberto Dessı́, Roberta Raileanu, Maria Lomeli, Eric Hambro,\n327\tLuke Zettlemoyer, Nicola Cancedda, and Thomas Scialom. Toolformer: Language models can\n328\tteach themselves to use tools. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, 2023. URL\n329\thttps://arxiv.org/abs/2302.04761.\n330\t[6] Matthew Wright and Adrian Freed. Open SoundControl: A new protocol for communicating with\n331\tsound synthesizers. In Proceedings of the International Computer Music Conference (ICMC), 1997.\n332\tURL https://opensoundcontrol.stanford.edu/files/1997-ICMC-OSC.pdf.\n333\t\n334\t5\n335\t\n336\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "5d6e7b31-8832-4b09-8cc5-4b945f4b766e", "uuid": "609e8c15-febc-4cc3-a726-13170e83a35d", "timestamp": "2026-07-29T05:34:15.198Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/llm4osc.txt", "content": "LLM4OSC: Profile-Bound Natural Language Control\nwith Deterministic Validation for Open Sound Control\nYuan-Yi Fan\nwww.yuanyifan.com\nLos Angeles\nyyf@yuanyifan.com\n\narXiv:2607.26024v1 [cs.HC] 28 Jul 2026\n\nAbstract\nOpen Sound Control (OSC) is the dominant wire protocol for real-time parametric control in professional audio, live performance, and virtual production. Large language models can emit plausible\nOSC, but they hallucinate addresses, mishandle type tags, and fail under paraphrase—unacceptable\nin show-critical contexts. We present LLM4OSC, a local-first architecture in which models propose structured intent JSON over a human-reviewed device profile, and deterministic code validates,\nclamps, and encodes before any UDP send. We introduce a frozen evaluation harness with CI gates\non wrong-send rate: mismatches that would still pass validation and transmit. On a Max/MSP hero\nprofile (12 patterns; 8 literal + 8 paraphrase + 4 refusal cases), after profile tag enrichment, symbolic\nslot fill, NL refine, and a retrieval confidence gate, backends B0–B3 all pass frozen gates (100%\nsemantic accuracy, 0% wrong-send). B0 (rules) remains the production default at ∼0.05 ms; LLM\nbackends remain ∼3–4 s. Historical few-shot B2 accuracy of 62.5% rises to 100% on this suite\nonly after symbolic post-processing—not because the 0.5B model alone becomes show-safe. We\nargue for propose–validate–send and wrong-send rate as first-class metrics for language-to-control\nsystems.\n\nKeywords. Open Sound Control, tool use, structured generation, safety-critical HCI, local inference,\nparametric control, creative AI.\n\n1\n\nIntroduction\n\nOSC [6] encodes real-time control as UDP messages: an address path (e.g. /gain), type tags (e.g.\nf), and typed arguments. Max/MSP, Ableton Live, QLab, TouchDesigner, Unreal Engine, and lighting\nconsoles each expose distinct address tables. Bridging operator intent to correct bytes does not scale\nwith show complexity.\nMCP2OSC [1] shows that LLMs coupled with structured tool interfaces can drive parametric control.\nProduction use additionally demands: (1) reproducibility (same command → same bytes); (2) closedworld safety (no invented addresses); (3) graceful refusal; (4) local operation; (5) paraphrase robustness.\nWe treat NL→OSC as schema-constrained resolution over a versioned device profile:\ndevice profile P + utterance u → intent JSON I (proposed)\n→ validate → clamp → encode → OSC\n\nContributions. (1) Intent and device-profile JSON contracts with manual provenance; (2) Tier 3 deterministic validate/clamp/encode/send; (3) Four NL backends (B0–B3) plus NL refine and a retrieval gate\non the LLM path; (4) An evaluation harness with wrong-send rate and literal–paraphrase gap (Track C);\n(5) An open MIT replication package with frozen goldens, CI, and scorecards.\n\n1\n\n\f2\n\nRelated Work\n\nOSC [6] remains the standard wire protocol for real-time music and multimedia control. MCP2OSC [1]\ndemonstrates parametric control by natural language via structured tool interfaces (NeurIPS Creative AI\nTrack). Parallel work on tool-using LMs [5] shows models can learn when and how to call APIs; we\ninstead bind proposals to a closed device profile and refuse out-of-world calls.\nWe frame warm-path pattern selection as lightweight retrieval over the profile catalog, in the spirit\nof retrieval-augmented generation [3], but with token-overlap ranking rather than dense retrievers. NL\nrefine and the retrieval gate follow the propose–then-revise pattern of Self-Refine [4], except that feedback and overrides come from deterministic profile policy (tag scores, slot fill, refusal reasons), not from\nanother LM pass. We extend the MCP2OSC line with an auditable send gate, versioned profiles, frozen\nwrong-send evaluation, and local inference. For optional domain adaptation we use LoRA [2] on synthetic profile-bound (NL, intent) pairs filtered through Tier 3, holding out frozen goldens. We optimize\nwrong-send rate: mismatches that pass validation and would transmit.\n\n3\n\nSystem\n\n3.1\n\nDevice Profile and Intent\n\nA profile P = (D, V, M) binds device id D, version V , and patterns M. Each pattern defines\npattern id, OSC address, type tags, slots, ranges, and retrieval metadata (tags, description).\nProfiles are human-committed; runtime binds to an explicit profile version. The profile is the\nclosed world: no address outside M may be sent.\nAn intent is either a success (kind:intent with pattern fields and args) or a refusal (kind:refusal\nwith enumerated reason). Reasons include unknown pattern, ambiguous pattern, missing slot,\nand out of range.\n\n3.2\n\nTier 3 Execution\n\nEngine E : (P, I) → bytes | reject validates device/version and pattern consistency, clamps numeric args, encodes OSC deterministically, and sends UDP. Design invariant: probabilistic components\npropose; Tier 3 decides and sends. No ML sits between intent JSON and wire bytes.\n\n3.3\n\nRetrieval, Refine, and Gate\n\nPatterns are ranked by token overlap: tags (weight ×3) plus description [3]. Top-k=8 patterns form\nLLM context; the same ranking drives B0.\nSlot fill deterministically parses percents, word numbers (half→ 0.5), note names (A440), pancenter phrases, and transport keywords from the utterance.\nNL refine (B1–B3). After the LLM proposes JSON [5], retrieval may override pattern id when\na higher-scoring pattern dominates, and slot fill re-extracts args from the original NL [4]. Refine runs on\nall LLM backends, not only LoRA.\nRetrieval gate (B1–B3). After refine, apply retrieval gate reapplies B0 policy: top score\n0 → unknown pattern; tied tops → ambiguous pattern; unfillable required slots → missing slot;\nchosen pattern with score 0 → refuse. This closes OOV wrong-sends (e.g. “boost the bass band by 3db”).\n\n3.4\n\nBackends and LoRA\n\nTable 1 summarizes backends. LoRA training expands templates over P (∼270 train / 67 val rows),\nexcludes frozen goldens, requires Tier 3-pass labels, and fine-tunes with r=8, α=16, one epoch on\nApple MPS. Adapter weights are gitignored; a model card records the recipe.\n\n2\n\n\fTable 1: NL backends. Refine and gate apply to B1–B3.\nID\n\nMechanism\n\nRole\n\nB0\nB1\nB2\nB3\n\nRetrieval + slot fill\nQwen2-0.5B zero-shot + refine + gate\nB1 + 8 few-shot + refine + gate\nB1 + LoRA + refine + gate\n\nProduction default\nPre-train floor\nPrompt ceiling\nOptional fine-tune\n\nTable 2: Track C overview (post-gate). All backends pass frozen gates. Harness: demo backend=b0,\nlora recommended=false.\nBackend\nLit.\nPara. Gap WS (para)\np50\nB0\nB1+refine+gate\nB2+refine+gate\nB3+refine+gate\n\n4\n\n100%\n100%\n100%\n100%\n\n100%\n100%\n100%\n100%\n\n0\n0\n0\n0\n\n0%\n0%\n0%\n0%\n\n0.05 ms\n∼3.7 s\n∼4.0 s\n∼3.6 s\n\nEvaluation\n\nDevice: Max/MSP profile prof 20260610 mvp0 (12 patterns). Suites: 8 literal NL, 8 paraphrase\nNL, 4 refusals (unknown, OOV EQ, missing slot, ambiguous). Default llm4osc score suite is\nliteral+refusal (CI gate); paraphrase is Track C.\nMetrics: semantic accuracy (match on pattern id, address, args); wrong-send rate (mismatch\nand Tier 3 dry-run would succeed); refusal recall; reproducibility; latency p50/p95. Release gate:\nwrong-send = 0% and semantic accuracy ≥ 90%. Gap:\ngapθ = (accliteral − accparaphrase ) × 100.\n\n5\n\nResults\n\nNumbers from frozen scorecards generated 2026-07-07 (track c.json), after tags/slots, NL refine,\nand the retrieval gate. CI: 111 pytest cases pass (2 skipped).\n\n5.1\n\nWhy B2 Moved from 62.5% to 100%\n\nThe 2026-06-28 Track C baseline scored raw B2 (few-shot → intent → Tier 3) at 62.5% literal / 62.5%\nparaphrase with non-zero wrong-send. Three symbolic changes explain the jump (Table 3):\nParaphrase recovery is driven by tags/slots + refine (no refusals in that suite). Literal gates require the\nretrieval gate: without it, LLM backends (including LoRA) still wrong-sent on OOV/ambiguous/missingslot goldens. On this small closed suite, post-LLM policy can make B1/B2 match B3 accuracy—a feature of the architecture, not evidence that raw 0.5B few-shot is show-safe. Latency still favors B0 by\n∼105 ×.\n\n5.2\n\nHistorical Ablation and Suite Detail\n\nLoRA-only (no refine) reached paraphrase 62.5% with 37.5% wrong-send—the same failure class as\nearly B2 until symbolic refine. Literal suite (8 NL + 4 refusal) and paraphrase suite (8 NL) both show\n100% semantic accuracy and 0% wrong-send for B0–B3 after the gate; refusal recall is 100% on the\nliteral suite.\n\n3\n\n\fTable 3: Causes of the B2 accuracy/gate recovery.\nChange\n\nEffect on B2\n\nProfile tags + slot\nparsers\nNL refine on B1–B3\n\nUsable retrieval; args independent of LLM unit parsing\n\nRetrieval gate\n\nOverrides wrong pattern id; re-fills args → paraphrase 62.5% → 100%\nAligns refusals with B0 → literal gates pass\nTable 4: Historical snapshots.\n\n5.3\n\nSnapshot\n\nB2 L/P\n\nGates\n\nNotes\n\n2026-06-28\n2026-06-29\n2026-07-07\n\n62.5/62.5\n—\n100/100\n\nfail\n—\npass\n\nRaw LLM\nB0 100%/100%; B3 refusals fail\n+ retrieval gate\n\nFindings\n\nF1. Wrong-send rate discriminates backends before policy: historical B2 beat B1 on accuracy but still\nwrong-sent. F2. Profile engineering can close paraphrase without LoRA (lora recommended=false).\nF3. Symbolic refine explains the B2 jump from 62.5% to 100% on this suite. F4. The retrieval gate is\nrequired for refusal safety on the LLM path. F5. Equal accuracy ̸= equal production choice: prefer B0\nfor live control on latency.\n\n6\n\nDiscussion\n\nLLM4OSC treats NL control as interface compilation over a closed API. On a tiny frozen suite, policy\n(profile + refine + gate) can dominate weights (few-shot / LoRA). Put truth in the contract; measure\nwrong-sends; refuse when retrieval is empty.\nLimitations: small benchmark (8+8+4, one device); profile-engineering confound; refine+gate can\nmask weak LLM proposals; LLM latency ∼3–4 s vs < 50 ms target; no user study; synthetic LoRA data\n(∼270 rows).\nFuture work: expand to 100–500+ stratified cases and a second device; ablate refine vs gate vs\nLoRA on larger OOV suites; wrong-send–aware training; MCP server surface; multi-arg/bundle schema\nv2.\n\n7\n\nConclusion\n\nWe presented LLM4OSC for profile-bound NL→OSC with deterministic Tier 3 validation. Wrong-send\nrate exposes unsafe successes that accuracy hides; enriched profiles and slot parsers can meet paraphrase\ngates without LoRA; historical few-shot B2 scores reach 100% only after NL refine; a retrieval confidence gate is required for refusal safety on the LLM path. B0 remains the live-control default. Code,\nschemas, goldens, and scorecards are released under MIT.\n\nData and Code Availability\nSource code, frozen goldens, scorecards, and evaluation documentation: https://github.com/\nyyf/LLM4OSC (MIT).\n\n4\n\n\fA\n\nSuccess Intent Example\n\n{\"schema_version\":\"1.0\",\"kind\":\"intent\",\n\"device_id\":\"max-msp\",\n\"profile_version\":\"prof_20260610_mvp0\",\n\"pattern_id\":\"gain_set\",\"address\":\"/gain\",\n\"type_tags\":\"f\",\"args\":[0.5]}\n\nB\n\nReproduction\n\ngit clone https://github.com/yyf/LLM4OSC\ncd LLM4OSC && python3 -m venv .venv\nsource .venv/bin/activate\npip install -e \".[dev]\"\npytest\n# 111 passed, 2 skipped\nllm4osc score\nllm4osc score --suite paraphrase\n\nFor Track C (B1–B3): pip install -e \".[dev,llm]\", then llm4osc serve and llm4osc\nscore-compare --backends b0,b1,b2,b3.\n\nReferences\n[1] Yuan-Yi Fan. MCP2OSC: Parametric control by natural language. In The Thirty-ninth Annual\nConference on Neural Information Processing Systems Creative AI Track: Humanity, 2025. URL\nhttps://openreview.net/forum?id=XLdFf7Zarw.\n[2] Edward J. Hu, Yelong Shen, Phillip Wallis, Zeyuan Allen-Zhu, Yuanzhi Li, Shean Wang, Lu Wang,\nand Weizhu Chen. LoRA: Low-rank adaptation of large language models, 2021. URL https:\n//arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685.\n[3] Patrick Lewis, Ethan Perez, Aleksandra Piktus, Fabio Petroni, Vladimir Karpukhin, Naman Goyal,\nHeinrich Küttler, Mike Lewis, Wen-tau Yih, Tim Rocktäschel, Sebastian Riedel, and Douwe Kiela.\nRetrieval-augmented generation for knowledge-intensive NLP tasks. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, 2020. URL https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401.\n[4] Aman Madaan, Niket Tandon, Prakhar Gupta, Skyler Hallinan, Luyu Gao, Sarah Wiegreffe, Uri\nAlon, Nouha Dziri, Shrimai Prabhumoye, Yiming Yang, Shashank Gupta, Bodhisattwa Prasad Majumder, Katherine Hermann, Sean Welleck, Amir Yazdanbakhsh, and Peter Clark. Self-refine: Iterative refinement with self-feedback. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, 2023.\nURL https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.17651.\n[5] Timo Schick, Jane Dwivedi-Yu, Roberto Dessı́, Roberta Raileanu, Maria Lomeli, Eric Hambro,\nLuke Zettlemoyer, Nicola Cancedda, and Thomas Scialom. Toolformer: Language models can\nteach themselves to use tools. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, 2023. URL\nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2302.04761.\n[6] Matthew Wright and Adrian Freed. Open SoundControl: A new protocol for communicating with\nsound synthesizers. In Proceedings of the International Computer Music Conference (ICMC), 1997.\nURL https://opensoundcontrol.stanford.edu/files/1997-ICMC-OSC.pdf.\n\n5\n\n\f", "numLines": 336, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 336}}}
